\section*{Single generation of separable-predual finite factors}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
All type-II\_1 factors with separable predual are claimed to have a single operator generator; the established direct-integral reduction then gives single generation for general separable-predual von Neumann algebras. The cited manuscript argues for that result; its relation to this problem is specified below.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
Manuscript 1 (family 296, 2026-09-23): Free independence lets a small perturbation accumulate along many occurrences in a long word, retaining a definite correlation with a chosen target. A free-group convolution bound controls the nonlinear remainder without growth in word length. Coordinate selection returns the ultrapower construction to the factor; density and a corner-and-matrix argument turn relative generation into one-operator generation.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
Manuscript 1 (family 296, 2026-09-23): The problem selects the mathematical statement summarized here, including its stated hypotheses and parameter range. The cited theorem gives a claimed proof of that assertion in full.

\subsection{Formal verification}
For family 296, the scope page documents the following selections. Selections give one-operator generation for factors with separable predual and dense relative unitary generators of irreducible inclusions in trace two-norm. No operator-norm separability is required. The documented statement covers the main claim of manuscript 1. Its selected declaration also covers the clause used for this problem. Comparator files: FactorGeneration.lean, RelativeGeneration.lean. Declarations: OAI.Generator.single\_generation\_of\_II1\_separable\_predual, OAI.RelativeGeneration.main\_theorem.

This site has not executed the Lean files or checked their dependencies. These descriptions report the scope of the linked declarations, rather than a new verification of the proofs.

\subsection{Status caveat}
OpenAI attributes these results to an internal model. Its release notice cautions that results lacking full formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently assessed the proofs; a solved label records the scope of the reported claim.

